FORMAT: 1A
HOST: https://api.flashy.app

# Flashy API Docs

Your account API key must be included in any request you send to Flashy API. You can send it in a few ways, choose the best one for you.

*   As a header in the request, the name of the header is "x-api-key" - Most recommended.
*   If you send a GET request you can pass the API key as query parameter "key=your_account_key".
*   If you send a request with body parameters you can include it as part of the request body as "key".
    

Before you can use our API, you must verify your Flashy account.

If you are using PHP we have a ready SDK wrapper - [PHP SDK](https://flashy.app/docs/php/).

* * *

*Before you can use our API, you must verify your Flashy account.*

## Account [/Account]

Read & manage your Flashy account information.

### Account [GET /account]

Get the current account.

+ Request (application/json)

+ Response 200 (application/json)

        {
                "success": true,
                "data": {
                        "id": 83,
                        "account": "r33piu",
                        "name": "My Account",
                        "country": null,
                        "website": "https://flashyapp.com",
                        "phone": "972526845430",
                        "credits": "24.0000",
                        "created_at": 1615565991,
                        "updated_at": 1631588013,
                        "timezone": "America/New_York",
                        "currency": "USD"
                }
        }




## Contacts [/Contacts]

Contacts are the foundation of the Flashy platform. You can create them based on any unique key that you prefer, with email being the default primary key.

We created a list of properties you can use to segment your contacts, and you can always create custom properties using our dashboard, read more about [Contact Properties](https://flashy.app/help/contact-management/utilizing-contact-properties/).

### Create [POST /contact]

Create or update contacts on Flashy by using the overwrite parameter.

Be sure to use the right Primary Key, our default is email address, but you can use any key you wish.

**You can add a contact to a specific list using the "lists" object, and the key is the List ID, and the value is true if the customer is marketing-eligible.**


+ Request (application/json)

    + Attributes
        + primary_key (required, string) - Name of the priamry key
        + overwrite (optional, boolean) - Update the contact if exists
        + contact (required, object) - Contact object
            + email (required, string) - Email
            + first_name (optional, string) - First name
            + last_name (optional, string) - Last name
            + birthday (optional, string) - Birthday

    + Body
        {
                "primary_key": "email",
                "overwrite": false,
                "contact": {
                        "email": "user1@name.com",
                        "first_name": "Rafael",
                        "last_name": "Mor",
                        "lists": {
                                "159": true,
                                "170": false
                        },
                        "birthday": 1631631569
                }
        }


+ Response 200 (application/json)

        {
                "success": true,
                "data": {
                        "email": "user1@name.com",
                        "first_name": "Rafael",
                        "last_name": "Mor",
                        "birthday": 1631631569,
                        "created_at": 1631631588,
                        "contact_id": "2291112565587e24668e171940cab7cc",
                        "lists": {}
                }
        }




### Update [PUT /contact/{contact_id}]

Update contact properties by the contact Primary Key.

Pay attention that the primary key of the contact needs to be part of the endpoint primary key to find a contact.

**Primary key can't be updated.**

+ Parameters
    + contact_id (required, string) - The contact identifier set on the endpoint URL
    + primary_key (optional, string) - The primary key to look the contacts based on, default is email

+ Request (application/json)

    + Attributes
        + contact (required, object) - The contact object with properties we want to update
            + last_name (optional, string) - The contact last name
            + source (optional, string) - The contact source
            + lists (optional, object) - Object of the lists and status of every list, key is the List ID and value is boolean

+ Response 200 (application/json)

        {
                "success": true,
                "data": {
                        "contact_id": "1a6711a68d0c381013ab72d3320ba4e5",
                        "email": "user1@name.com",
                        "birthday": 1631631569,
                        "created_at": 1632022656,
                        "phone": "",
                        "first_name": "Rafael",
                        "last_name": "Mor",
                        "source": "Google",
                        "lists": {}
                }
        }




### Get [GET /contact/{identifier}?primary_key={primary_key}]

Use your primary key to find a contact.

+ Parameters
    + identifier (required, string) - The contact identifier set on the endpoint URL
    + primary_key (optional, string) - The primary key to look the contacts based on, default is email

+ Request (application/json)


+ Response 200 (application/json)

        {
                "success": true,
                "data": {
                        "contact_id": "1a6711a68d0c381013ab72d3320ba4e5",
                        "email": "user1@name.com",
                        "birthday": 1631631569,
                        "created_at": 1632022656,
                        "phone": "",
                        "first_name": "Rafael",
                        "last_name": "Mor",
                        "lists": {}
                }
        }




### Example: Get By Contact ID [GET /contact/1a6711a68d0c381013ab72d3320ba4e5?primary_key=contact_id]

Use your primary key to find a contact.

+ Parameters
    + primary_key (optional, string) - The primary key to look the contacts based on, default is email

+ Request (application/json)


+ Response 200 (application/json)

        {
                "success": true,
                "data": {
                        "contact_id": "1a6711a68d0c381013ab72d3320ba4e5",
                        "email": "user1@name.com",
                        "birthday": 1631631569,
                        "created_at": 1632022656,
                        "phone": "",
                        "first_name": "Rafael",
                        "last_name": "Mor",
                        "lists": {}
                }
        }




### Example: Get By Email [GET /contact/user1@name.com?primary_key=email]

Use your primary key to find a contact.

+ Parameters
    + primary_key (optional, string) - The primary key to look the contacts based on, default is email

+ Request (application/json)


+ Response 200 (application/json)

        {
                "success": true,
                "data": {
                        "contact_id": "1a6711a68d0c381013ab72d3320ba4e5",
                        "email": "user1@name.com",
                        "birthday": 1631631569,
                        "created_at": 1632022656,
                        "phone": "",
                        "first_name": "Rafael",
                        "last_name": "Mor",
                        "lists": {}
                }
        }




### Block [POST /contact/{contact_id}/block?primary_key={primary_key}]

You can mark a contact as blocked whenever you don't want them to receive Flashy emails and SMS messages. The platform will then mark him as unsubscribed on all mailing lists he is a member of.

The contact can sign up again and the status will change to subscribed based on the list he signed up for.

+ Parameters
    + contact_id (required, string) - The contact identifier set on the endpoint URL
    + primary_key (optional, string) - The primary key to look the contacts based on, default is email

+ Request (application/json)




